  "Solution": {
    "S": "Let $I = \\int_{0}^{3} \\frac{dx}{(x+2)\\sqrt{x+1}}$.\n\nPut $x+1 = t^2 \\Rightarrow dx = 2t\\,dt$. When $x=0, t=1$; when $x=3, t=2$.\n\n$$I = \\int_{1}^{2} \\frac{2t\\,dt}{(t^2+1)\\sqrt{t^2}} = 2\\int_{1}^{2} \\frac{dt}{t^2+1} = 2[\\tan^{-1}t]_{1}^{2}$$\n\n$$= 2(\\tan^{-1}2 - \\tan^{-1}1) = 2\\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{2-1}{1+2}\\right) = 2\\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)$$\n\nThus the correct option is B."
  },
